  abstract = {The late 1990s and early 2000s witnessed the publication of a substantial body of scholarship exploring intersections and points of friction between medieval studies and postcolonial theory. Yet during this first phase of medieval postcolonialism, conceived here as ending around 2015, engagement with the larger field of postcolonial studies was limited in scope. While a handful of foundational theoretical texts proved immensely generative, significant contributions to this vast, heterogeneous field were overlooked. Connections between medieval and postcolonial studies have, moreover, expanded very little over the last decade, a period in which the ``postcolonial'' has been displaced by the ``global'' as the dominant framework for exploring premodern cross-cultural relations. This special issue introduces new postcolonial voices, paradigms, and analytical methods into medieval studies. Its goals in doing so are (1) to enlarge the conceptual and theoretical arsenal used by medievalists to understand cultural contact in the distant past; (2) to invite new ways of thinking about the relations between the medieval and modern; and (3) to advance urgent conversations on premodern race, Mediterranean history, and the global Middle Ages.},
