  abstract = {In the context of the rise of the far right, researchers have advanced comparative analytical tools for the identification of common characteristics that transcend both local contexts and the possible ideological divergences within these movements. The most influential analytical framework, the Populist Radical Right (PRR), has enabled researchers to map and compare far right movements across diverse political systems by highlighting their convergent components. However, Latin American cases reveal tensions between the framework's universalist ambitions and the specific contexts in which the far right manifests. Recognizing the analytical value of the PRR while addressing one of its major limitations---its Eurocentric bias---this article compares the European and Latin American far right and proposes a contextualization that incorporates the region's particular historical and social dimensions. In doing so, it advances debates that seek to understand the far right beyond European generalizations and contributes to filling the gap generated by scholarship centered on the global North.},
